<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yashwant Rao Holkar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a>–<a href="/years/1811">1811</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Yashwantrao Holkar I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holkars">Holkars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yashwant Rao Holkar was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> ruler of Indore from 1799 to 1811. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scindias">Scindias</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Poona</a> in 1802, and in 1804–1805 fought the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, routing Colonel Monson's column in 1804 before making peace at Rajghat in 1805.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yashwant-rao-holkar.jpg" alt="Yashwant Rao Holkar" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yashwant_Rao_Holkar_of_Indore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yashwant Rao was the third son of Tukoji Rao Holkar. He was educated in accountancy and could read Persian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>. On 29 January 1797 Tukoji named his eldest son, Kashi Rao, as his successor. The second son, Malhar Rao, rebelled and Yashwant Rao joined him; on 14 September the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulat-rao-scindia">Daulat Rao Scindia</a> surprised their camp, Malhar Rao was killed and Yashwant Rao fled wounded. He gathered a following that included Pindharis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> under Mir Khan. Claiming to act for his infant nephew Khande Rao, he declared himself head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holkars">Holkars</a> in January 1799, and by April had defeated Kashi Rao's commander, the Chevalier Dudrenec.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1799 the widows of Mahadaji Scindia asked his help to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> and promised him four lakh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>; he accepted five lakh from Ambaji Ingle to betray them, and looted the city. His victory over Scindia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> on 25 October 1802, the day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a>, was won at Hadapsar near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>. The Peshwa fled to Sinhagad, and Holkar took over the administration at Pune and appointed Amrutrao as Peshwa. The British, fearing for their own territories and those of their ally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>, decided to restore the Peshwa under their protection.</p><p>On 8 October 1804 he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> to gain control of the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II and besieged it for more than a week, but could not overcome the defence under Colonel Ochterlony. The emperor nevertheless gave him the title Maharajadhiraja Raj Rajeshwar Alija Bahadur. He appealed to other Indian rulers to join him against the British, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulat-rao-scindia">Daulat Rao Scindia</a> would not fight and others gave no direct help. He fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> refused a British request to arrest him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By the treaty signed at Rajghat on 24 December 1805 the British returned his territory, accepted his dominion over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, Kota and Bundi and undertook not to interfere in Holkar affairs; he gave up his claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a> and the lands north of the Chambal. He thus avoided the subsidiary alliance imposed on other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> chiefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-lake-1st-viscount-lake">Lord Lake</a> resigned in protest at the abandonment of the Company's Indian allies. In a letter of 15 February 1806 Holkar wrote that he had concluded the war when he found himself abandoned on all sides. He died on 28 October 1811.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yashwantrao Holkar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Yashwant Rao Holkar</b> fought the British in 1804–05, but after defeat at Mahidpur in 1817 the Treaty of Mandsaur made Indore a protected state, and the capital moved to Indore.</blockquote><a href="/state/indore-state">Indore State</a></li><li><blockquote>Amir Khan, a Pathan soldier of fortune, led a large army in alliance with <b>Yashwant Rao Holkar</b> of Indore State and plundered Rajputana for two decades.</blockquote><a href="/state/tonk-state">Tonk State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Bhanpura, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.42,75.54&amp;year=1798" aria-label="Malwa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6572 1336 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yashwant Rao Holkar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7022 1606h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7022 1606h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.42,75.54&amp;year=1798">What was here in 1798? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
